WEBVTT

00:00:00.010 --> 00:00:05.347
Partition pruning can help Spark avoid reading data that is not relevant to the query.

00:00:05.347 --> 00:00:11.807
Suppose a large table is organized by service date, and the query asks for only one month.

00:00:11.807 --> 00:00:17.527
If the storage layout supports it, Spark may skip partitions outside that month.

00:00:17.527 --> 00:00:24.132
That can be much more efficient than scanning the entire dataset and then discarding most of the rows.

00:00:24.132 --> 00:00:28.127
A partition column should match useful access patterns.

00:00:28.127 --> 00:00:34.540
A nearly unique identifier is often a poor choice because it can create too many tiny partitions.
